<body>

<section class="quote-screen dark" id="open-quote">
  <div class="quote-inner">
    <div class="quote-mark" id="q1-mark">&ldquo;</div>
    <div class="quote-text" id="q1-text">My name is Ashwin, and the Japanese government rejected me once. A year later, they gave me a scholarship anyway.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll-cue" id="scroll-cue">scroll</div>
</section>

<section id="hero">
  <div id="hero-inner">
    <p class="kicker">A talk for Amity law students, in the hall I once sat in</p>
    <h1 id="hero-h1">I almost didn't apply<br>to the thing that <span class="accent">changed my life.</span></h1>
    <p class="subtext" id="hero-sub">Not because I didn't want it. Because wanting it, out loud, felt riskier than not trying at all. If that sounds familiar, good, stay a while.</p>

    <div class="stamp-interactive reveal-stat" id="stamp-interactive" tabindex="0" role="button" aria-label="Tap to see what changed">
      <div class="stamp-face" id="stamp-face">REJECTED</div>
      <div class="stamp-sub" id="stamp-sub">Embassy Recommendation &middot; Year One</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stamp-hint">tap the stamp</div>

    <div class="hero-stats reveal-stat" id="hero-stats">
      <div class="hstat"><div class="hnum" data-target="1">0</div><div class="hlbl">day of margin<br>on the deadline</div></div>
      <div class="hstat"><div class="hnum" data-target="9">0</div><div class="hlbl">days the post<br>took to arrive</div></div>
      <div class="hstat"><div class="hnum" data-target="6167">0</div><div class="hlbl">km, Amity<br>to Fukuoka</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="btn-wrap" id="btn-wrap"><button class="btn" id="begin-btn">Begin</button></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="s1">
  <div class="eyebrow reveal">Chapter one</div>
  <h2 class="reveal">The person sitting in this room</h2>
  <p class="reveal">I'm Ashwin. Some of you know me as an alumnus. Most of you don't know me at all, and that's fine, I didn't know the guy who spoke at my orientation either. I studied law here at Amity, went to Japan on a Japanese Government MEXT scholarship, did my LL.M. at Kyushu University, worked there for a few years, and came back to India.</p>
  <p class="reveal marg" id="p-marg-1">I'm supposed to talk about higher education and studying abroad. But I don't want to start with Japan. I want to start here, because before Japan, before MEXT, before any of it, I was just a student in this building, as unsure as you probably are right now.<span class="hand-note">— genuinely, not a metaphor</span></p>

  <div class="exhibit reveal">
    <div class="obj-name">Old Amity ID card</div>
    <div class="obj-desc">Bad photo. Worse haircut. Kept it anyway, you don't throw out the evidence.</div>
  </div>

  <p class="reveal">I wanted to do something with my life. I wanted to be good at what I did. I wanted to see something outside India. But if you'd asked me to draw the exact roadmap, I couldn't have. Nobody hands you that roadmap. You build it two or three steps at a time, mostly in the dark.</p>
  <p class="reveal">We put a stupid amount of pressure on twenty-year-olds to have a five-year plan. I didn't. I had a "get through this semester, then figure out the next thing" plan, and that turned out to be enough.</p>

  <div class="card-grid reveal">
    <div class="flip-card" tabindex="0" role="button">
      <div class="front-text">A five-year plan.</div>
      <div class="back-text">Some of you have one. Genuinely, respect.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="flip-card" tabindex="0" role="button">
      <div class="front-text">A five-week plan.</div>
      <div class="back-text">This was closer to my actual reality.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="flip-card" tabindex="0" role="button">
      <div class="front-text">"Let me survive this semester."</div>
      <div class="back-text">Also a plan. Nobody tells you this counts.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="hint reveal">tap one</p>
  <p class="lede reveal" style="margin-top:28px;">Ambition and certainty are not the same thing. You're allowed to have one without the other.</p>
</section>

<section class="band" id="s2">
  <div class="eyebrow reveal">Chapter two</div>
  <h2 class="reveal">Then college got cancelled, sort of</h2>

  <div class="zoom-wrap reveal">
    <div class="zoom-grid" id="zoom-grid"></div>
    <div class="zoom-cap">One of these was me. Camera off, muted, occasionally still in bed.</div>
    <div class="zoom-hint">tap tiles to toggle cameras</div>
  </div>

  <p class="reveal marg" id="p-marg-2">COVID hit midway through, and whatever normal college life we had just disappeared overnight. Classrooms became screens. You could attend a full day of lectures without changing out of pyjamas, and most of us did, repeatedly.<span class="hand-note">— be honest, you did too</span></p>

  <div class="timeline reveal">
    <div class="step"><div class="dot filled"></div><div><div class="step-label">Before</div><div class="step-sub">Campus, friends, the version of college you actually signed up for.</div></div></div>
    <div class="step"><div class="dot filled"></div><div><div class="step-label">2020</div><div class="step-sub">The world said "nope, different plan now." Nobody asked us.</div></div></div>
    <div class="step"><div class="dot filled"></div><div><div class="step-label">After</div><div class="step-sub">Same question waiting on the other side: okay, now what?</div></div></div>
  </div>
  <p class="reveal">A lot of you in this room carry some version of that. Your plans got rewritten by something you had zero control over. That does something to how you trust plans afterward, worth naming, not just moving past.</p>
</section>

<section id="s3">
  <div class="eyebrow reveal">Chapter three</div>
  <h2 class="reveal">Let's just say it: Amity is not your excuse</h2>
  <p class="lede reveal">Does the name on my degree decide what I get to do next?</p>
  <p class="reveal">I know exactly what people say about this place. "Amity gives everyone placements." "Amity is a business, not a college." "Pay the fee, get the degree." I've heard every version of that joke, at parties, in comment sections, from relatives who studied at colleges nobody's heard of either but somehow feel entitled to an opinion. I made a few of those jokes myself, back when I needed something else to blame for my own uncertainty.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Here's the part nobody says out loud: some of that criticism isn't even wrong. There probably are people in this hall who picked their electives by which professor takes attendance the least. That's real. I'm not going to stand up here and tell you Amity is secretly Oxford.</p>

  <p class="pull reveal">But an easy college is not the same thing as an easy ceiling. <span class="accent">Nobody hands you that ceiling. You build it yourself, one lazy semester at a time, if you let them.</span></p>

  <p class="reveal">I sat in the exact seats you're sitting in and told myself a version of "well, it's just Amity" more than once. It's a comfortable sentence. It lets you stop trying and still feel like the system failed you instead of the other way round. I believed it right up until MEXT didn't ask where I studied before it asked what I actually knew. Nobody at that embassy interview said "oh, Amity, say no more." They asked about my research proposal, my grades, my letters, and whether I could speak for myself for fifteen straight minutes without notes. That's it. That's the whole test.</p>

  <div class="exhibit reveal">
    <div class="obj-name">Organisational Behaviour, class notes</div>
    <div class="obj-desc">Dipanwita Ma'am's class. I was not thinking, "this will matter someday." I was thinking about the exam and, honestly, lunch.</div>
  </div>

  <p class="reveal">You don't know in advance which class is going to matter later. That's the annoying part. So take the boring, unglamorous classes seriously too, not because anyone's watching, but because future-you doesn't get to choose what they'll need.</p>

  <p class="pull reveal">Stop asking whether Amity is good enough for the world. <span class="accent">Start asking whether you're using it properly.</span> Those are two completely different questions, and only one of them is actually about you.</p>
</section>

<section class="band" id="s4">
  <div class="eyebrow reveal">Chapter four</div>
  <h2 class="reveal">Then I found Japan, by accident</h2>
  <p class="reveal">No childhood dream here. I wasn't the kid drawing torii gates in the margins. I got curious, one random evening, and fell down a very normal rabbit hole of reading and videos until 1am, which I generously called "research" the next morning.</p>
  <p class="reveal">A different legal system. A different academic culture. The chance to live somewhere genuinely unfamiliar and do real research there. It stopped being a passing interest and started being a plan I was actually building toward.</p>

  <div class="illus-strip reveal">
    <div class="illus-cell">
      <svg width="46" height="46" viewBox="0 0 46 46"><circle cx="23" cy="23" r="21" stroke="#0a0a0a" stroke-width="1.5" fill="none"/><path d="M23 6v34M6 23h34" stroke="#b23b30" stroke-width="1.2"/></svg>
      <div class="cap">Different legal system</div>
    </div>
    <div class="illus-cell">
      <svg width="46" height="46" viewBox="0 0 46 46"><rect x="8" y="14" width="30" height="22" rx="2" stroke="#0a0a0a" stroke-width="1.5" fill="none"/><path d="M8 20h30" stroke="#b23b30" stroke-width="1.2"/></svg>
      <div class="cap">Different research culture</div>
    </div>
    <div class="illus-cell">
      <svg width="46" height="46" viewBox="0 0 46 46"><path d="M8 34 L23 10 L38 34Z" stroke="#0a0a0a" stroke-width="1.5" fill="none"/><circle cx="23" cy="26" r="2.4" fill="#b23b30"/></svg>
      <div class="cap">Somewhere unfamiliar</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="exhibit reveal">
    <div class="obj-name">The Japanese Government (MEXT) Scholarship</div>
    <div class="obj-desc">Plainly: the Japanese government funding international students to study there, tuition, monthly stipend, travel, via the embassy or university-recommendation route.</div>
  </div>

  <p class="reveal">Here's the part I usually skip and shouldn't: this wasn't my first attempt. A year earlier, I'd applied through the embassy route and didn't make it. Written test, interview at the consulate, the whole thing, and I got a polite no.</p>
  <p class="pull reveal">Embassy Recommendation is the harder door. <span class="accent">More applicants chase fewer seats, and a single bad interview day can end it.</span> Nobody tells you that going in.</p>
  <p class="reveal">So the version of this story you're about to hear is actually round two, and it went through the university-recommendation route instead, a professor at Kyushu willing to vouch for me, not the embassy screening me cold. Different door, same building.</p>

  <p class="lede reveal">But don't hear "fully funded" and switch your brain off. The money is a reason to look closer. It's a terrible reason to choose your entire education.</p>
</section>

<section id="s5">
  <div class="eyebrow reveal">Chapter five</div>
  <h2 class="reveal">The application I almost didn't send</h2>
  <p class="reveal">Deadline: third week of January. And here's the honest bit, part of why I nearly didn't bother: I'd already been rejected once, by the harder route, the year before. It's very easy to tell yourself "I already know how this ends" and just not show up a second time.</p>
  <p class="reveal">I started days before the deadline anyway. Not "documents mostly ready" days before, actively-still-writing-the-proposal days before.</p>
  <p class="reveal marg" id="p-marg-3">I know how that sounds. It was exactly that irresponsible. I'm not telling you this as a strategy. <span class="hand-note">please don't copy this bit</span></p>

  <div class="checklist reveal" id="checklist">
    <div class="check-item no"><span class="mark">✕</span> Started months early</div>
    <div class="check-item no"><span class="mark">✕</span> A calm, orderly process</div>
    <div class="check-item no"><span class="mark">✕</span> Everything printed and ready</div>
    <div class="check-item yes"><span class="mark">✓</span> Sent it anyway, shaking slightly</div>
  </div>

  <p class="reveal">This wasn't a form you submit online and forget. Documents had to be printed, numbered, arranged in the right order, and couriered, physically, to the university, to reach them thousands of kilometres away, on time.</p>

  <div class="receipt-wrap reveal" id="receipt-wrap">
    <div class="receipt">
      <div class="r-stamp">1 DAY TO SPARE</div>
      <div class="r-head">INTL. COURIER RECEIPT</div>
      <div class="r-row"><span>ORIGIN</span><b>NEW DELHI, IN</b></div>
      <div class="r-row"><span>DESTINATION</span><b>FUKUOKA, JP</b></div>
      <div class="r-row"><span>CONTENTS</span><b>APPLICATION DOCS</b></div>
      <div class="r-row"><span>SERVICE</span><b>INTL. EXPRESS</b></div>
      <div class="r-row"><span>EST. TRANSIT</span><b>7&ndash;10 DAYS</b></div>
      <div class="r-row"><span>STATUS</span><b>DISPATCHED</b></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="exhibit reveal">
    <div class="obj-name">The courier receipt</div>
    <div class="obj-desc">Handed over exactly one day before the post deadline. Zero margin. The courier clerk actually asked if I was sure this was going to make it. I said yes. I was not sure.</div>
  </div>

  <p class="reveal">The documents took seven to ten days to reach Japan. Once they left my hands, there was nothing more I could do, which is a strange kind of peace. You've done the thing. Whatever happens next isn't yours to control anymore.</p>

  <div class="panic-wrap reveal">
    <label>How close have you cut a deadline like this?</label>
    <input type="range" id="panic-slider" min="0" max="100" value="50">
    <div class="panic-scale"><span>weeks early</span><span>the night before</span><span>the hour before</span></div>
    <div id="panic-readout">Move it. Be honest with yourself.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band" id="s6">
  <div class="eyebrow reveal">Chapter six</div>
  <h2 class="reveal">Then, nothing, for months</h2>
  <div class="month-tracker reveal" id="month-tracker">
    <div class="mt-months">
      <span class="mt-m active" data-i="0">JAN</span>
      <span class="mt-m" data-i="1">FEB</span>
      <span class="mt-m" data-i="2">MAR</span>
      <span class="mt-m" data-i="3">APR</span>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-readout" id="mt-readout">Sent it. No tracking number, no update, no way to check in.</div>
  </div>
  <p class="hint reveal">tap a month</p>
  <p class="reveal">This is the part nobody warns you about. You send something out and then you just... live with the not-knowing. You replay the proposal in your head at odd hours. You wonder if you should've written that one paragraph differently. You assume, quietly, that it probably didn't work.</p>
  <p class="pull reveal">And then, because life doesn't pause for your anxiety, <span class="accent">you go back to attending classes and forget about it. Mostly.</span></p>
</section>

<section id="s7">
  <div class="eyebrow reveal">Chapter seven</div>
  <h2 class="reveal">April</h2>
  <p class="reveal">The result came in April. I opened it expecting a no, I'd already written the graceful version of that story in my head, the one where "it wasn't meant to be" and I moved on with dignity. I didn't need that version.</p>
</section>

<section class="stat-break">
  <div class="sb-num">SELECTED</div>
  <div class="sb-label">MEXT Scholarship &middot; April</div>
</section>

<section id="s7b">
  <div class="exhibit reveal">
    <div class="obj-name">Kyushu University acceptance letter</div>
    <div class="obj-desc">Filed next to the courier receipt. One is proof of the risk. The other is proof it paid off.</div>
  </div>

  <p class="lede reveal">You can't lose an opportunity you never applied for, but you also can't win it. The only guaranteed outcome of not trying is nothing. Every time.</p>
</section>

<section class="band" id="s8">
  <div class="eyebrow reveal">Chapter eight, Fukuoka</div>
  <h2 class="reveal">Landing, and immediately drowning a little</h2>
  <p class="reveal">I'd researched Japan for months. Watched every video, read every blog. None of it prepared me for actually having to live there, alone, on day one, unable to read a single sign around me.</p>

  <div class="exhibit reveal">
    <div class="obj-name">Student ID, Kyushu University</div>
    <div class="obj-desc">Issued in the first week, back when everything, the campus map, the vending machines, my own address, still felt foreign.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="flap-wrap reveal">
    <div class="flap-row" id="flap-row">
      <div class="flap">HAKATA</div>
    </div>
    <button class="flap-btn" id="flap-btn">Tap to relive the wrong platform</button>
    <div class="flap-cap" id="flap-cap"></div>
  </div>

  <p class="reveal">The first month broke me a little, honestly. New systems, new logistics, a language I barely spoke, and for the first time being responsible for every single decision with no one to check with. Even the washing machine felt like it was testing me, and for the record, it won the first round.</p>

  <div class="struggle-grid reveal">
    <div class="struggle-card" tabindex="0" role="button">
      <div class="title">Grateful and struggling, both at once <span class="plus">+</span></div>
      <div class="body">We think gratitude cancels out struggle, that if you're lucky enough to be there, you're not allowed to find it hard. That's wrong. I was genuinely grateful. I was also, some weeks, quietly overwhelmed. Neither one made the other one a lie.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="struggle-card" tabindex="0" role="button">
      <div class="title">Getting properly, embarrassingly lost <span class="plus">+</span></div>
      <div class="body">Wrong train, twenty minutes in the wrong direction, walking back through a station I didn't recognise, holding groceries, questioning every decision that put me there.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="struggle-card" tabindex="0" role="button">
      <div class="title">Starting from zero, socially <span class="plus">+</span></div>
      <div class="body">Nobody there cared what my transcript said. You rebuild your entire social footing from scratch, on day one, in a language you're still learning.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="struggle-card" tabindex="0" role="button">
      <div class="title">Homesickness that doesn't announce itself <span class="plus">+</span></div>
      <div class="body">It rarely shows up as "I miss home." It shows up as irritability over something tiny and unrelated, until you notice what's actually underneath it.</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="s9">
  <div class="eyebrow reveal">Chapter nine</div>
  <h2 class="reveal">Japan, minus the filter</h2>
  <div class="split reveal">
    <div class="expect">
      <h4>What travels well online</h4>
      <ul><li>Cleanliness</li><li>Punctuality</li><li>Safety</li><li>Public transport</li><li>Convenience stores</li><li>Food, nature, the universities</li></ul>
    </div>
    <div class="real">
      <h4>What doesn't make the reel</h4>
      <ul><li>The language wall</li><li>Loneliness</li><li>Misread social cues</li><li>Doing everything alone</li><li>Feeling like an outsider</li><li>Learning to actually ask for help</li></ul>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="lede reveal" style="margin-top:30px;">The first few weeks, taking a single train could feel like a small exam I hadn't studied for.</p>
</section>

<section id="s9b">
  <div class="eyebrow reveal">Chapter nine, continued</div>
  <h2 class="reveal">One year, three part-time jobs, and a full-time offer</h2>
  <p class="reveal">The LL.M. at Kyushu was one year. Twelve months to learn a legal system, a language, and how to be useful to someone, all at the same time. I didn't spend it just attending lectures.</p>

  <div class="ladder reveal" id="ladder">
    <div class="ladder-step" tabindex="0" role="button">
      <div class="ladder-dot">1</div>
      <div class="ladder-body">
        <div class="ladder-title">Part-time role, law firm no. 1</div>
        <div class="ladder-detail">Mostly research and translation grunt work. I was slow, over-formal, and said "yes, understood" to instructions I'd only half understood. Everyone starts here.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="ladder-step" tabindex="0" role="button">
      <div class="ladder-dot">2</div>
      <div class="ladder-body">
        <div class="ladder-title">Part-time role, law firm no. 2</div>
        <div class="ladder-detail">Better fit, harder work. This is where the Amity habit of actually reading the whole judgment, not just the headnote, started paying rent.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="ladder-step" tabindex="0" role="button">
      <div class="ladder-dot">3</div>
      <div class="ladder-body">
        <div class="ladder-title">Part-time role, law firm no. 3</div>
        <div class="ladder-detail">By now I wasn't the intern who needed everything explained twice. People started routing actual work to me instead of busywork.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="ladder-step final" tabindex="0" role="button">
      <div class="ladder-dot">✓</div>
      <div class="ladder-body">
        <div class="ladder-title">Full-time offer</div>
        <div class="ladder-detail">One of those three firms made it permanent. Not because I was the most brilliant person in the room, I wasn't, but because I'd shown up consistently enough for long enough that hiring me was the boring, obvious choice.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="hint reveal">tap each step</p>

  <p class="pull reveal">Nobody at that firm ever asked which Indian college I went to. <span class="accent">They asked if I could get the work done by Friday.</span></p>
  <p class="reveal">The honest through-line, the writing discipline, the habit of actually finishing what I started, the willingness to redo something badly done, all of that traces back further than Japan. It traces back to a law degree at Amity that I could have coasted through and mostly chose not to.</p>
</section>

<section class="band" id="s10">
  <div class="eyebrow reveal">Chapter ten</div>
  <h2 class="reveal">What actually stayed with me</h2>
  <p class="reveal">Not "resilience" or "leadership", those are CV words, the ones you reach for when the honest answer feels too small to say out loud. What Japan actually gave me: noticing other people, cleaning up after myself without being told, asking for help without shame, appreciating routine instead of resenting it.</p>

  <div class="lesson-block reveal">
    <div class="lesson-num">The one piece of advice I'd bet on</div>
    <div class="lesson-title">Be a decent person. That's it. That's the whole strategy.</div>
    <div class="quote-block">Hold the door. Say thank you and mean it. Don't treat someone differently because of what job they do. Help when you can. Apologise when you're wrong. Remember the people who helped you.</div>
    <p>None of that gets you a scholarship on its own. But it decides what kind of person ends up getting one, and what kind of person is worth being around after they do. We spend so much energy trying to look impressive that we forget being pleasant is the rarer, harder skill.</p>
  </div>

  <p class="pull reveal">Talent gets you shortlisted. <span class="accent">Being someone people actually want to keep around is what gets you hired, kept, and recommended, again and again.</span></p>
</section>

<section id="s11">
  <div class="eyebrow reveal">Chapter eleven</div>
  <h2 class="reveal">What you should actually do, starting now</h2>
  <p class="pull reveal">This is the gyan section. <span class="accent">You've earned the story part. Here's the useful part.</span></p>
  <div class="advice-list reveal">
    <div class="advice-item"><div class="n">01</div><div><div class="h">Take your degree seriously.</div><p>"I'll learn it later" is a promise most people quietly break.</p></div></div>
    <div class="advice-item"><div class="n">02</div><div><div class="h">Read outside the syllabus.</div><p>If something interests you, chase it. Nobody needs to assign it first.</p></div></div>
    <div class="advice-item"><div class="n">03</div><div><div class="h">Learn to write clearly.</div><p>Thinking well and communicating that thinking are two different skills. Law lives on the second one.</p></div></div>
    <div class="advice-item"><div class="n">04</div><div><div class="h">Stop collecting certificates for the feed.</div><p>One project you can actually explain beats ten you can't.</p></div></div>
    <div class="advice-item"><div class="n">05</div><div><div class="h">Apply. Just apply.</div><p>I almost didn't. Ask me how close it was, I'll tell you the honest version.</p></div></div>
    <div class="advice-item"><div class="n">06</div><div><div class="h">Talk to your professors before you need something.</div><p>Relationships built only for recommendation letters read exactly like that.</p></div></div>
    <div class="advice-item"><div class="n">07</div><div><div class="h">Be someone people remember kindly.</div><p>Long after your marks are forgotten, how you treated people isn't.</p></div></div>
    <div class="advice-item"><div class="n">08</div><div><div class="h">Stop comparing starting points.</div><p>Someone else's college name isn't your ceiling. It's just their starting line.</p></div></div>
    <div class="advice-item"><div class="n">09</div><div><div class="h">Let yourself be ambitious.</div><p>Humility and small ambitions are not the same thing. Don't confuse them.</p></div></div>
    <div class="advice-item"><div class="n">10</div><div><div class="h">Stay decent, always.</div><p>It's the least impressive-sounding advice in this whole talk. It's also the one I'd bet my life on.</p></div></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band" id="tips">
  <div class="eyebrow reveal">A practical detour</div>
  <h2 class="reveal">If you're actually applying to MEXT, here's the real checklist</h2>
  <p class="reveal">Everything above was the feeling of it. This part is the mechanics, the stuff I wish someone had told me plainly instead of me figuring it out at 2am, days before my own deadline. I looked up the current cycle's requirements before writing this so it's not just me misremembering 2018.</p>

  <div class="pull reveal">There are two ways in. I've now been rejected by one and accepted through the other, so let's actually talk about the difference.</div>

  <div class="route-tabs reveal">
    <div class="route-tab">
      <span class="rt-letter">A</span>
      <span class="rt-name">Embassy<br>Recommendation</span>
    </div>
    <div class="route-tab active">
      <span class="rt-letter">B</span>
      <span class="rt-name">University<br>Recommendation</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="split reveal">
    <div class="expect">
      <h4>Embassy Recommendation — tougher, and the one that rejected me first</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>You apply through the Japanese embassy or consulate in your own country. In my case, an actual physical visit and a very official-looking waiting room.</li>
        <li>The embassy screens you first: documents, then a written test, then an interview, and only then forwards the strongest names to MEXT.</li>
        <li>Far more applicants chase far fewer seats here. One rough interview day can end the whole thing, which is exactly what happened to me the first time.</li>
        <li>You pick your preferred university and field yourself, but final placement isn't fully in your hands, MEXT and the university both have a say.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="real">
      <h4>University Recommendation — the one that actually worked</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>You apply straight to a Japanese university, who screens you and nominates you directly to MEXT. The embassy isn't involved at all.</li>
        <li>Usually means you've already been in touch with a professor there who's willing to supervise you, its own small project before the "real" application even starts.</li>
        <li>Gives you more certainty about where you'll land, since the university that nominates you is the one you'll study at.</li>
        <li>Fewer people even know this route exists, which, unfairly, works a little in your favour.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="reveal" style="margin-top:26px;">I didn't plan it this way. I tried embassy route first because it's the one everyone's heard of, got rejected, and only found out about university route afterward, mostly by accident, while emailing professors about something else entirely. If you're reading this early enough to try both doors on purpose, you're already ahead of where I was.</p>
  <p class="reveal">One honest warning: don't apply to both at once in the same cycle thinking it improves your odds. It doesn't stack, it just quietly doubles your paperwork and your stress, and I promise you have enough of both already.</p>

  <div class="tips-grid reveal">
    <div class="tip-card">
      <svg class="tip-icon" viewBox="0 0 34 34" fill="none"><circle cx="17" cy="12" r="6" stroke="#0a0a0a" stroke-width="1.6"/><path d="M6 29c0-6 5-10 11-10s11 4 11 10" stroke="#b23b30" stroke-width="1.6"/></svg>
      <div class="tip-title">Ask for recommendation letters weeks early</div>
      <div class="tip-body">You'll typically need <b>two academic references</b>. Give your recommenders real notice, not a Friday-night WhatsApp message for a Monday deadline, and send them a short note on what you'd like them to highlight. A rushed letter reads rushed.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="tip-card">
      <svg class="tip-icon" viewBox="0 0 34 34" fill="none"><rect x="4" y="10" width="26" height="16" rx="2" stroke="#0a0a0a" stroke-width="1.6"/><path d="M4 15h26" stroke="#b23b30" stroke-width="1.6"/></svg>
      <div class="tip-title">"Fully funded" needs a footnote</div>
      <div class="tip-body">MEXT waives tuition and entrance fees for selected students and covers round-trip economy airfare and a monthly stipend. It generally does <b>not</b> cover a spouse or kids travelling with you, or your visa fee. Check the fine print before you plan your whole life around the headline number.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="tip-card">
      <svg class="tip-icon" viewBox="0 0 34 34" fill="none"><path d="M17 4l4 8 9 1-6.5 6 1.5 9-8-4.5-8 4.5 1.5-9L4 13l9-1z" stroke="#0a0a0a" stroke-width="1.4" fill="none"/></svg>
      <div class="tip-title">Your research proposal doesn't need to be perfect</div>
      <div class="tip-body">It needs to be <b>coherent, and honest about what you don't know yet.</b> Expect several drafts, mine went through more revisions than I'd like to admit. Screeners can tell a genuine question apart from a polished-sounding one padded with jargon.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="tip-card">
      <svg class="tip-icon" viewBox="0 0 34 34" fill="none"><path d="M6 26L17 6l11 20z" stroke="#0a0a0a" stroke-width="1.6" fill="none"/><path d="M17 15v6" stroke="#b23b30" stroke-width="1.8"/><circle cx="17" cy="23" r="1.2" fill="#b23b30"/></svg>
      <div class="tip-title">Grades matter, but not the way you think</div>
      <div class="tip-body">There's no official minimum GPA, but competitive files usually sit around <b>80% or a 3.0/4.0 equivalent</b>. A strong record in your actual field can outweigh a mediocre score in something unrelated. So stop panicking about that one elective you bombed in first year.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="tip-card">
      <svg class="tip-icon" viewBox="0 0 34 34" fill="none"><rect x="6" y="6" width="22" height="22" rx="11" stroke="#0a0a0a" stroke-width="1.6"/><path d="M17 11v6l4 3" stroke="#b23b30" stroke-width="1.8"/></svg>
      <div class="tip-title">Number every document, literally</div>
      <div class="tip-body">Applications typically want documents submitted as <b>one original plus copies</b>, each one numbered by hand in the top corner, in the exact order the checklist specifies. It sounds fussy because it is. It's also exactly the kind of thing an embassy official rejects an application over.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="doc-checklist reveal">
    <div class="doc-row"><div class="num">1</div><div><span class="dname">Application form</span><span class="dnote">From the embassy site. Fill in every field, even the ones that feel optional.</span></div><div class="dtag">form</div></div>
    <div class="doc-row"><div class="num">2</div><div><span class="dname">Research plan / study plan</span><span class="dnote">Start six weeks out if you can. Expect three to five drafts, minimum.</span></div><div class="dtag">essay</div></div>
    <div class="doc-row"><div class="num">3</div><div><span class="dname">Academic transcripts</span><span class="dnote">Official copies from your university. These take longer to issue than you'd think.</span></div><div class="dtag">official</div></div>
    <div class="doc-row"><div class="num">4</div><div><span class="dname">Graduation certificate</span><span class="dnote">Or a letter confirming expected graduation date if you haven't finished yet.</span></div><div class="dtag">official</div></div>
    <div class="doc-row"><div class="num">5</div><div><span class="dname">Two recommendation letters</span><span class="dnote">Academic references. Ask at least four weeks before you need them.</span></div><div class="dtag">people</div></div>
    <div class="doc-row"><div class="num">6</div><div><span class="dname">Health certificate</span><span class="dnote">On MEXT's own form, from a doctor. Book the appointment early, clinics get booked up.</span></div><div class="dtag">medical</div></div>
    <div class="doc-row"><div class="num">7</div><div><span class="dname">Passport copy + photograph</span><span class="dnote">Recent, passport-style. Don't use the one from your ninth-grade ID card.</span></div><div class="dtag">ID</div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="budget-wrap reveal" style="margin-top:44px;">
    <p class="lede" style="margin-bottom:6px;">Roughly where my prep time actually went</p>
    <p style="font-size:14px; color:var(--grey); font-family:'Inter',sans-serif; text-align:left; margin-top:0;">Not the plan I had. The plan I actually lived, in hindsight.</p>
    <div class="budget-row">
      <div class="lbl">SOP + proposal</div>
      <div class="budget-track"><div class="budget-fill" data-pct="40"></div></div>
      <div class="pct">40%</div>
    </div>
    <div class="budget-row">
      <div class="lbl">Chasing documents</div>
      <div class="budget-track"><div class="budget-fill" data-pct="25"></div></div>
      <div class="pct">25%</div>
    </div>
    <div class="budget-row">
      <div class="lbl">Panicking</div>
      <div class="budget-track"><div class="budget-fill" data-pct="20"></div></div>
      <div class="pct">20%</div>
    </div>
    <div class="budget-row">
      <div class="lbl">Actually printing</div>
      <div class="budget-track"><div class="budget-fill" data-pct="15"></div></div>
      <div class="pct">15%</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="reveal" style="font-size:13px; color:var(--grey); font-family:'Inter',sans-serif; text-align:left;">Requirements shift a little each cycle and vary by embassy, so treat this as orientation, not gospel. Confirm exact documents and dates on your own embassy's current notice before you do anything else.</p>
</section>

<section id="s12">
  <div class="eyebrow reveal">Chapter twelve</div>
  <h2 class="reveal">Coming home hurt more than leaving did</h2>
  <p class="reveal">Nobody warns you about this part. Everyone prepares you for culture shock going out. Nobody prepares you for the version coming back in.</p>
  <p class="reveal">The first week back, I got irrationally angry at traffic that hadn't changed at all, I had. I flinched at noise I grew up in. I caught myself queuing at a counter that had no queue, standing there like an idiot while people walked past me, because four years of Japan had rewired something in my brain that India was never going to match, and honestly, didn't need to.</p>

  <p class="pull reveal">And then there was the question. You know the one. <span class="accent">"So why did you come back?"</span></p>
  <p class="reveal">Asked with a smile that doesn't quite reach the eyes. Asked by relatives calculating salary differences in their head. Asked by people who half-expect the answer to be "I couldn't make it out there," and are almost disappointed when it isn't.</p>
  <p class="reveal">Here's my honest answer, the one I actually give now: family, and a genuine pull toward building something here, in a market I understand, for people I understand. That's it. It's not a consolation prize. It's a decision, made with the same seriousness I made the decision to leave in the first place. I don't owe anyone a more impressive reason than that, and neither do you, whichever way you eventually go.</p>

  <div class="exhibit reveal">
    <div class="obj-name">IC card, Fukuoka</div>
    <div class="obj-desc">Tapped onto every train for a few years. Sitting now next to the Amity ID it started beside, because apparently that's how this story was always going to end, back where it began.</div>
  </div>

  <p class="pull reveal">I'm not here to tell you everyone should go abroad. <span class="accent">Some of you should. Some shouldn't. Both of those are correct answers, and only you get to decide which one is yours.</span></p>
</section>

<section class="band" id="ama-section">
  <div class="eyebrow reveal">Ask me anything</div>
  <h2 class="reveal">Questions students actually ask me</h2>
  <div class="ama reveal">
    <div class="ama-item">
      <button class="ama-q"><span>Embassy route or university route, which one should I do?</span><span class="plus">+</span></button>
      <div class="ama-a"><p>If you already have a Japanese professor willing to supervise you, university route is genuinely the easier door. Embassy route is tougher, more applicants for fewer seats, and it's the one that rejected me the first time I tried. I got in the second year, through university route instead.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ama-item">
      <button class="ama-q"><span>How hard is MEXT, honestly?</span><span class="plus">+</span></button>
      <div class="ama-a"><p>Hard enough to filter for genuine intent, not so hard a hardworking normal student can't do it. Paperwork discipline matters as much as brilliance, which is funny, given how I sent mine in.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ama-item">
      <button class="ama-q"><span>Did you know Japanese before you left?</span><span class="plus">+</span></button>
      <div class="ama-a"><p>Barely a word. Most of it came from necessity, in the first year, out of sheer trial and error, and a lot of pointing at menus.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ama-item">
      <button class="ama-q"><span>Is an LL.M. abroad actually useful for Indian law?</span><span class="plus">+</span></button>
      <div class="ama-a"><p>It's useful precisely because it's not a repeat of what you already learned here. Different systems teach you to question assumptions you didn't know you had.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ama-item">
      <button class="ama-q"><span>Was it lonely?</span><span class="plus">+</span></button>
      <div class="ama-a"><p>Yes, especially early on. It got easier the day I stopped comparing my new life to my old one and just let the new one be its own thing.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ama-item">
      <button class="ama-q"><span>Do I need to already know what field I want to research?</span><span class="plus">+</span></button>
      <div class="ama-a"><p>You need a direction, not a finished thesis. My proposal changed shape twice after I got to Fukuoka. That's normal, and honestly expected.</p></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ama-item">
      <button class="ama-q"><span>Why come back to India at all?</span><span class="plus">+</span></button>
      <div class="ama-a"><p>Family, mostly, and a pull toward building something here. Simple to say in one line. Took me a lot longer than one line to actually get to it.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="pre-final">
  <div class="eyebrow reveal">The last thing</div>
  <p class="reveal">When I sat where you're sitting, I assumed the people who "had it figured out" were just built differently from me. They weren't. Most people you admire are just making calls with half the information, and hoping the call was right.</p>

  <div class="reveal" style="margin-top:34px; font-family:'Source Serif 4',serif; font-size:16px; color:var(--grey); line-height:2; text-align:left;">
    I applied days before the deadline.<br>
    I couriered my documents one day before the cut-off.<br>
    I waited seven to ten days for them to reach Japan.<br>
    I got the result in April.<br>
    I landed in Fukuoka and lost, badly, to a washing machine.<br>
    And then, slowly, the unfamiliar just became my life.
  </div>

  <p class="reveal" style="margin-top:34px;">That's the best description I have for adulthood, honestly. Nobody hands you a moment where it all clicks. You just keep going until the unfamiliar quietly becomes familiar.</p>

  <div class="deadline-box reveal">
    <label>Type the thing you've been putting off</label>
    <input type="text" id="deadline-input" placeholder="e.g. that scholarship application">
    <div id="deadline-out"></div>
  </div>

  <div style="text-align:center;"><button class="btn" id="confetti-btn" style="margin-top:8px;">Send it</button></div>
</section>

<section class="quote-screen dark" id="close-quote">
  <div class="quote-inner">
    <div class="quote-mark" id="q2-mark">&ldquo;</div>
    <div class="quote-text" id="q2-text">Ten years from now, you won't remember the deadline you missed sleep over. You'll remember whether you sent it.</div>
    <div class="quote-attr" id="q2-attr">So, what are you going to apply for?</div>
  </div>
</section>

<footer>A talk about one application, filed late, that changed a life.<br>(Ashwin, still not a planner.)</footer>

